1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,174
ReverseX Engineer is an AI-powered software investigation platform that explores how application features behave, reconstructs them, and independently verifies the results.

2
00:00:11,174 --> 00:00:17,411
It works against authorized local demo applications—not arbitrary websites on the open internet.

3
00:00:17,411 --> 00:00:22,608
This short walkthrough uses verified local results from a completed Guided Demo.

4
00:00:22,958 --> 00:00:29,092
A user selects an allowlisted application—here, TaskBoard—and chooses Understand and Reconstruct.

5
00:00:29,092 --> 00:00:39,400
For this demonstration we open a Guided Demo investigation that was already completed and verified, rather than starting a new live Meta Muse run during recording.

6
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:50,404
In Muse mode, the agent can choose browser interactions such as fill, click, and reload, while ReverseX records observations into an evidence-backed behavioral specification.

7
00:00:50,404 --> 00:01:00,902
The job on screen ran in Guided Demo mode with zero Meta API requests in that release pass, so you can trust the stages without paying for a fresh Muse investigation.

8
00:01:01,252 --> 00:01:10,088
From the frozen behavior specification, ReverseX produces an independent implementation as static files—HTML, JavaScript, and CSS.

9
00:01:10,088 --> 00:01:14,233
The original TaskBoard source never enters the model context.

10
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:19,943
In this verified run, reconstruction was reused from an existing Phase Two artifact.

11
00:01:19,943 --> 00:01:32,788
We are therefore showing prior reconstruction evidence and status labels from that completed job, not implying that new reconstruction files were generated live during this video recording.

12
00:01:33,138 --> 00:01:40,171
ReverseX then compares the original application with the reconstruction using independent behavioral tests.

13
00:01:40,171 --> 00:01:46,284
Playwright exercises holdout scenarios against both sides so the model does not grade itself.

14
00:01:46,284 --> 00:01:52,397
For TaskBoard, five of five scenarios passed—a one hundred percent match on the tested cases.

15
00:01:52,397 --> 00:01:58,050
That score applies to those scenarios, not every possible behavior of the application.

16
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:07,217
The Simple Report explains the investigation in plain language for nontechnical readers—what was tested, what matched, and key limits.

17
00:02:07,217 --> 00:02:16,232
Switching to the Developer Report keeps the same verified findings, and adds technical observations, stage details, and testing evidence.

18
00:02:16,232 --> 00:02:22,088
Both reports draw from one completed investigation record, so the story stays consistent.

19
00:02:22,438 --> 00:02:27,123
Next is the Controlled Repair Example—a labeled fault-injection evaluation.

20
00:02:27,123 --> 00:02:36,556
A deliberate defect was introduced into a reconstruction copy, detected by the oracle, then corrected through a bounded repair attempt and revalidated.

21
00:02:36,556 --> 00:02:44,614
This is separate from the TaskBoard investigation you just reviewed, and it is not an organic AI failure from that completed job.

22
00:02:44,964 --> 00:02:54,083
ReverseX demonstrates investigate, reconstruct, independently validate, and controlled repair—with evidence you can audit.

23
00:02:54,083 --> 00:02:55,428
ReverseX Engineer.

